// C fills memory of no type through `out void *` only as an array the binding names or a struct
// mirror; memory of no type C allocates, through `out void **`, is answered as bytes; and a `void *`
// passed in whose length nothing carries is pointed at the forms that carry one, in bytes.
// error: out value 1 of the C binding `scalar`, `p`, is `long` in Avin and `void` in C: C writes no value through `out void *p`, which points at memory of no type: pass an array or a `ByteBlock` for C to write, as in `void *p, length(p) size_t n`, or answer what C fills, an array named with `array: "p"` and its length in bytes given with `length:`, or a struct mirror
// error: out value 1 of the C binding `text`, `p`, is `string` in Avin and `void` in C: C writes no value through `out void *p`, which points at memory of no type
// error: out value 1 of the C binding `wide`, `p`, is `Array[long]` in Avin and `void *` in C: C allocates memory of no type for `p`, whose length in bytes need not be a whole number of `long`s, so it is answered as an `Array[byte]`
// error: parameter 1 of the C binding `unbounded` is `Array[int]` in Avin and `void *` in C: C could read past the end of an array whose length nothing carries: mark the integer parameter that carries it `length(p)`, or, for memory C fills with as many bytes as the binding can work out before the call, write it `out void *p` and name it with `array: "p"`, its length in bytes a C expression given with `length:`
// error: parameter 1 of the C binding `pointers` is `long` in Avin and `void * *` in C: `void * *` points at memory of no type

@c("int f(out void *p)")
scalar() -> (int, long)

@c("int f(out void *p)")
text() -> (int, string)

@c("int g(out void **p, out int *n)", array: "p", length: "n", free: "free")
wide() -> (int, Array[long], int)

@c("int h(void *p)")
unbounded(p: Array[int]) -> int

@c("int k(void **p)")
pointers(p: long) -> int

main() = 0
